      ">>> Stage 1: Proposition Constructive Speech...\n",
      "\n",
      "[ PROPOSITION OPENING ]\n",
      "**Opening Speech – Proposition**\n",
      "\n",
      "Ladies and gentlemen, esteemed judges, and fellow scholars, the proposition stands unequivocally that researchers should employ large language models (LLMs) as control groups when gathering data and subsequently compare those results to human participants. In an era where scientific inquiry demands ever‑greater volumes of data, the traditional reliance on human control groups is increasingly untenable. LLMs offer **unmatched scalability**: a single model can generate millions of responses across countless experimental conditions in the time it would take a human cohort to complete a single session. This capacity enables researchers to explore a breadth of variables—cultural contexts, linguistic nuances, demographic profiles—without the logistical bottlenecks of recruitment, scheduling, and attrition that cripple conventional studies.\n",
      "\n",
      "Beyond sheer scale, LLMs deliver **cost‑efficiency and reproducibility** that no human control group can match. Recruiting, compensating, and managing participants entails substantial financial outlays and administrative overhead, often limiting sample sizes and introducing budget‑driven biases. By contrast, once an LLM is trained, each additional datum costs virtually nothing, and the same model can be re‑run under identical parameters to verify findings, ensuring that results are not artifacts of a particular participant pool. This standardized baseline testing eliminates the variability inherent in human samples—fatigue, mood swings, misunderstandings—providing a consistent, noise‑reduced reference point against which human performance can be meaningfully assessed.\n",
      "\n",
      "Finally, modern LLMs have demonstrated an extraordinary ability to **simulate aggregate human behavioral patterns**. Trained on diverse corpora encompassing millions of texts from varied cultures, ages, and expertise levels, these models internalize statistical regularities that mirror collective human cognition, language use, and decision‑making. When used as control groups, LLMs act as a synthetic “average human,” allowing researchers to isolate the unique contributions of individual differences, interventions, or experimental manipulations. By juxtaposing human data against this calibrated, reproducible baseline, we gain clearer insight into what truly sets human cognition apart, while simultaneously accelerating discovery, conserving resources, and upholding the rigorous standards of scientific methodology. For these compelling reasons—scalability, cost‑effectiveness, standardization, reproducibility, and faithful simulation of aggregate human behavior—we affirm that LLMs should be embraced as control groups in contemporary research.\n",
      "\n",
      ">>> Stage 2: Opposition Opening Speech...\n",
